## Base hierarchy (create in `:core-ui` / `:presentation` when scaffolding)

```
ParentFragment / ParentActivity   ← ViewBinding inflate + clear on destroy
  └── BasePermissionFragment      ← runtime permission / GPS helpers (presentation)
        └── BaseFragment          ← shared injects (prefs, ads, billing) if project needs them
              └── Feature Fragment
```

Also provide when needed: `ParentDialog`, `ParentBottomSheet` (and dismissal variants) in `:core-ui`.

Rules:
- Feature screens **extend** the project's existing base — do not reinvent ViewBinding lifecycle
- Binding is only valid between `onCreateView`/`onViewCreated` and `onDestroyView` — never cache Binding in a ViewModel
- Prefer constructor/`bindingFactory` pattern from Parent* over manual inflate in every screen

## Flow collection (core-ui extensions)

## Flow collection (core-ui extensions)

- UI State: Collect `StateFlow` using `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)` (or a shared `collectWhenStarted` extension) to ensure collection only happens while the UI is visible.
- UI Effects/Events: Collect one-time events using `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)` as well. Use `SharedFlow`/event patterns with proper replay or buffering configuration when events must not be lost.
- Keep lifecycle-aware Flow collection extensions inside `:core-ui` and reuse them across modules instead of duplicating `lifecycleScope.launch { repeatOnLifecycle { collect } }` blocks.

## Feature Fragment responsibilities

- `initObservers()` / collect state + effects
- Dispatch Intents only — no business logic
- Call permission helpers via Effects from ViewModel when needed
- Navigate with NavController only in UI layer

## Forbidden

- Holding View / Binding / Fragment references in ViewModel
- `findViewById` / Data Binding
- Inflating layouts in ViewModel
- Skipping Parent* and managing Binding lifecycle ad hoc in every screen